**Theorem 2.4 (full actual raw-row square).** Under Assumption 1.2 the
finite table $\Pi_U$, initialized as below, realizes every actual native
row of $\mathcal R$ by one patch. It has simultaneous enabledness with
the native partial operation, the same label, actual supplied/emitted
bit, owner/role/origin and guard, and

$$
\operatorname{step}_{U}(\Gamma_U(W(h)),b)
   =\Gamma_U(W(hb))
$$

whenever the prescribed native action $b$ executes. An unexecuted illegal
operation has no patch and no receipt. An enabled malformed/raw exit has
its real successor. Original and added boot, acquisition, partial control
and data phases and the original final halt edge are all in this statement.

Proof. Each fixed native store has a finite number of slots. Hence its
entire raw bit-pattern space, not merely its intended table, is finite.
PC and decoded field ranges are finite. The maximum effect-list length
and the union of its named local roles over this finite space are finite.
If a raw store does not decode, its main row is absent exactly as specified
in ARC Definition 6.2. If it does decode, its actual finite ordered local
effect is one of the enumerated cases. No estimate of three old cells is
used: a CTL can name several current/predecessor markers on several ports.
The finite maximum of these actual lists supplies the bound instead.

The outer scaffold has finite height, all head links have length one,
and a current/predecessor/neighbor route has bounded extra length. A cell
component has bounded height and the finite-location component has at
most $3q-1$ vertices. Thus every enumerated route, inspected component,
assignment list and birth list has a fixed bound. One may take the
maximum of their explicitly enumerated walk lengths and list sizes;
neither table finiteness nor locality requires an unproved numerical
radius. The primitive's actual cursor relation is preserved by each
head operation. An ill-typed row or failed native cursor/boundary guard
is absent, rather than repaired into a different head configuration.

For the local equality, consider each native core.

| Core | Equality of decoded effect and native effect |
| --- | --- |
| RD | Decode the same actual marked symbol into the named latch and take its identical literal equality-test successor. |
| WR | With the same writer/writability guard, assign only the designated symbol tracks, from the actual literal or latched variant. Raw invalid source bits remain raw. |
| ML/MR | Test the same existing boundary/neighbor, change the same two cursor marks and retarget the corresponding access link. |
| EX | At ordinary R retain that old cell identity and head, change it to blank, and append the new unmarked R; do not move the ordinary head to the new R. |
| BF | Use the identical eight-entry three-input truth table and designated result subfield. |
| IN | Consume the same bit at the actual installed site and change only its input latch and native control/receipt fields. No tape write occurs. |
| OUT | Release precisely the permitted actual latch bit. No new observation of a private coordinate is introduced. |
| TOK | Test source=1 and destination=0 in the same named finite availability slots and perform their same two updates. Same principal does not remove these rows. |
| CTL | Apply the actual finite effect list and testing/successor convention, including all its named marker footprints. Commitment interprets prepared data; it does not construct an unbounded object. |
| Boundary/bit boot variant | Construct the one native boundary cell or one finite location with its actual value and cursor successor. An absent bit is constructed by the nine-assignment template. |

Every row then performs its native receipt. Change the old append R into
the selected ordinary/F receipt, append a new meter R, and move only the
append mark. Preserve the core's newly updated meter read mark. If the
read and append heads or any named roles coincide, compute their combined
native result on that one old byte before assigning it. Its two head
links occupy distinct endpoint lanes. Core meter RD/moves retain their
actual effect; no core EX/WR permission on the meter is invented. Partial
boot words and head activation use the same existing-cell/constructor
cases. All reciprocal assignments in Definition 2.2 yield precisely
these final decoded fields. This proves the row square and the identical
guard failure at every actual pre-row record.

At ordinal zero endow $F_{75}$, the $F_q$ optional scaffold with exactly
the actually endowed primitive bits present, the two original meter
boundary cells and its two links, and empty other tape words. Charge
this finite foundation and its rule apparatus as in §4. It decodes to
the native irreducible entrance. Run every original boundary, private
IN/WR/move, validation, stationary acquisition, control installation,
and every ARC activation row through its own patch. In particular the
twenty new role-boundary constructors, both bootstraps, captured
$\Delta(G)$, empty-bank prelude and asset imports are executed, not
preloaded by the graph map. Wrong raw bootstrap supply remains stored
while its bootstrap is active; after DONE the enumeration uses the actual
decoded table. Unique well-typed noncanonical rows are covered by the
same local cases. No success or return of such a raw table is asserted.

Induction from that foundation gives the equation at every finite
actual prefix. Loan cuts, unavailable descriptors, partial copies and
source-invalid cuts use their literal tracks and tokens throughout.
The original source is neither replaced nor restored from a second
source. The last original halt CTL has the same output and final receipt
and enables no later entry. This completes the initialized row proof. ∎

